<p class="lead">Search engine optimization is not a single task. It is a collection of disciplines — technical, creative, analytical, and strategic — that work together to move your website up in Google search results. If you are a business owner evaluating SEO services for the first time, the landscape can feel overwhelming. This guide breaks down exactly what SEO services include, what you should expect to pay, and how to tell a legitimate provider from a scam.</p>

        <h2>What Does an SEO Company Actually Do?</h2>
        <p>An SEO company exists to do one thing: increase the quantity and quality of organic traffic that reaches your website from search engines. That sounds simple, but the work behind it spans multiple disciplines and takes months to execute properly.</p>
        <p>Most reputable SEO agencies begin with a comprehensive website audit. This audit examines your site structure, page speed, mobile usability, content quality, keyword targeting, backlink profile, and technical crawl issues. The audit becomes the foundation for every decision that follows. Without it, any SEO work is guesswork.</p>
        <p>From there, the agency moves into keyword research. This is not about finding words with high search volume. It is about finding the search terms your actual customers use when they are ready to buy. A good agency analyzes search intent — informational, commercial, or transactional — and maps keywords to specific pages on your site.</p>
        <p>On-page optimization comes next. This includes rewriting title tags and meta descriptions, improving heading structure, optimizing images with alt text, strengthening internal linking, and ensuring each page has clear, useful content that satisfies the search query. Technical SEO runs in parallel: fixing site speed issues, resolving crawl errors, implementing HTTPS security, optimizing for mobile, and submitting XML sitemaps.</p>
        <p>Content creation is where many campaigns differentiate themselves. SEO-focused content includes blog posts, service pages, landing pages, and resource guides — all built around keywords that drive qualified traffic. The best agencies do not just write content. They build topical authority by creating clusters of related content that signal expertise to Google.</p>
        <p>Off-page SEO, primarily link building, remains a critical ranking factor. Agencies earn backlinks through guest posts, digital PR, partnerships, and local citations. For local businesses, this also includes <a href="/blog/google-business-profile-guide/">Google Business Profile</a> optimization, review management, and directory submissions.</p>
        <p>Finally, reporting and analytics tie everything together. A professional SEO company tracks rankings, organic traffic, conversions, and return on investment. You should never be left wondering whether your money is working.</p>

        <h2>The Six Core Types of SEO Services</h2>
        <p>Not every business needs every type of SEO. Understanding the six core categories helps you match services to your actual needs instead of buying a bloated package.</p>

        <h3>1. On-Page SEO</h3>
        <p>On-page SEO covers every element on your website that you can directly control. This includes title tags, meta descriptions, header tags (H1 through H6), image alt text, internal links, URL structure, and the actual content on each page. In 2026, on-page SEO has shifted from keyword density to intent satisfaction. Google does not count how many times you use a keyword. It measures whether the page answers the user's question completely.</p>
        <p>Key on-page tasks include optimizing title tags to stay within 60 characters while including the primary keyword naturally, writing meta descriptions that earn clicks from search results, using a logical heading hierarchy that helps users and search engines scan the page, and adding <a href="/blog/schema-markup-checklist/">schema markup</a> to qualify for rich snippets like star ratings, FAQs, and how-to panels.</p>

        <h3>2. Off-Page SEO</h3>
        <p>Off-page SEO covers everything that happens outside your website but affects your rankings. The dominant factor is backlinks — links from other websites pointing to yours. Google treats backlinks as votes of confidence. The more high-quality, relevant sites that link to you, the more authoritative your site appears.</p>
        <p>Off-page work includes earning backlinks through guest posting on industry blogs, digital PR campaigns that earn coverage in news outlets, reclaiming unlinked brand mentions, building relationships with complementary businesses for partnership links, and submitting your site to relevant directories and resource pages. For local businesses, off-page SEO also includes local citation building — consistent mentions of your business name, address, and phone number across the web.</p>

        <h3>3. Technical SEO</h3>
        <p>Technical SEO is the foundation everything else sits on. If your site has technical problems, no amount of content or links will save it. Technical SEO ensures search engines can crawl, index, and render your pages efficiently.</p>
        <p>Core technical tasks include optimizing Core Web Vitals — Largest Contentful Paint, Interaction to Next Paint, and Cumulative Layout Shift — to meet Google's performance thresholds, fixing broken links and redirect chains that waste crawl budget, implementing proper canonical tags to prevent duplicate content issues, ensuring mobile-friendliness across all devices, creating and submitting XML sitemaps, managing robots.txt to guide crawler behavior, and implementing structured data markup for enhanced search results.</p>

        <h3>4. Local SEO</h3>
        <p>Local SEO is a specialized subset focused on businesses that serve a specific geographic area. If you are a plumber, dentist, contractor, or med spa, <a href="/blog/local-seo-guide/">local SEO</a> is often more valuable than national rankings. <a href="/blog/seo-for-local-businesses/">Read our complete local SEO guide for the step-by-step playbook.</a></p>
        <p>Local SEO services include Google Business Profile optimization with complete fields, regular posts, and photo updates, local keyword targeting that includes city and neighborhood names, citation building across directories like Yelp, Bing Places, Apple Maps, and industry-specific sites, review generation and reputation management, and location page creation for each area you serve.</p>

        <h3>5. Content SEO</h3>
        <p>Content SEO is the practice of creating and optimizing content specifically to rank in search results and convert visitors. This goes beyond basic blogging. It is a strategic discipline that maps content to the buyer journey.</p>
        <p>Content SEO services include keyword-focused content briefs that define what a piece must cover to outrank competitors, pillar page and topic cluster development that builds topical authority, existing content refreshes that update outdated posts with new data and expanded coverage, conversion-focused landing pages for high-intent keywords, and FAQ and resource content that captures featured snippets and voice search queries.</p>

        <h3>6. SEO Reporting and Analytics</h3>
        <p>Reporting is not an afterthought. It is how you know whether your investment is working. Professional SEO reporting goes beyond vanity metrics like rankings and traffic. It connects SEO activity to business outcomes.</p>
        <p>Standard reporting includes keyword ranking tracking for your target terms, organic traffic analysis with segmentation by landing page and geography, conversion tracking for form submissions, phone calls, and purchases, backlink monitoring to track new and lost links, technical health scores from crawl audits, and competitor benchmarking to measure your progress against rivals.</p>

