        <p>Be wary of any provider promising page one rankings for $299 per month. Quality SEO requires skilled labor, quality tools, and time. Prices below $500 per month typically indicate automated or outsourced work that carries risk. Google penalties from bad SEO can take months or years to recover from.</p>

        <h2>How Long Does SEO Take to Work?</h2>
        <p>This is the question every business owner asks, and the honest answer is: it depends. Most SEO campaigns begin showing measurable results within 60 to 90 days. Significant ranking improvements typically appear between months three and six. For competitive industries in large markets, it can take 6 to 12 months to reach page one for high-value keywords.</p>
        <p>The timeline depends on several factors. New domains start from zero authority and take longer than established sites. Highly competitive keywords in major cities require more effort than niche terms in smaller markets. Websites with severe technical debt need cleanup before growth work can begin. Businesses that publish content consistently outperform those that pause after the first month.</p>
        <p>The key is patience combined with consistency. SEO is not a campaign with a start and end date. It is an ongoing investment that compounds over time. The businesses that treat it as a long-term strategy are the ones that dominate search results year after year.</p>

        <h2>DIY SEO vs Hiring an Agency: What Is the Right Choice?</h2>
        <p>Some business owners wonder whether they can handle SEO internally. The answer depends on your resources, expertise, and time. <a href="/blog/diy-seo-vs-hiring-an-agency-pros-and-cons/">Read our full comparison of DIY SEO versus hiring an agency.</a></p>
        <p>DIY SEO is possible if you have a marketing-savvy team member with 10 to 20 hours per week to dedicate to it. You will need to learn keyword research tools, content optimization techniques, technical site management, and link building outreach. The advantage is cost savings. The disadvantage is speed. Learning curves are steep, and mistakes can hurt your site.</p>
        <p>Hiring an agency makes sense when SEO is critical to your growth strategy and you need results faster than an internal team can deliver. Agencies bring experience, established processes, professional tools, and a team of specialists. The trade-off is cost — but for most businesses, the speed and quality of professional execution pays for itself through faster ranking improvements and higher conversion rates.</p>
        <p>A hybrid approach also works. Some businesses hire an agency for strategy and technical work while handling content creation internally. Others use an agency for the first 6 to 12 months to build momentum, then transition to in-house management. There is no single right answer — only the answer that fits your business.</p>

        <h2>Red Flags: How to Spot a Bad SEO Provider</h2>
        <p>The SEO industry has its share of bad actors. Knowing the warning signs protects you from wasted money and potential Google penalties.</p>
        <p>Guaranteed rankings are the biggest red flag. No one can guarantee a number one ranking on Google. Rankings depend on hundreds of factors, many outside any agency's control. Any provider making absolute promises is either lying or planning to use manipulative tactics.</p>
        <p>Instant results claims are equally suspicious. Legitimate SEO takes months. Anyone promising page one in 30 days is likely using black-hat techniques like keyword stuffing, cloaking, or private blog networks. These tactics can produce short-term gains followed by devastating penalties.</p>
        <p>Lack of transparency is another warning sign. A professional agency explains what they are doing and why. They provide regular reports with actual data. If a provider refuses to share their strategy, their link sources, or their work history, walk away.</p>
        <p>Finally, watch for cookie-cutter packages. Every business has different needs, competition levels, and goals. A real agency customizes its approach. A factory-style provider selling the same package to every client is not doing strategic work.</p>

        <h2>What to Expect in the First 90 Days</h2>
        <p>Understanding the typical SEO timeline helps you evaluate progress and avoid unrealistic expectations. Here is what a professional engagement looks like month by month.</p>
        <p>In month one, the agency conducts a full technical audit, competitor analysis, and baseline rankings report. They identify critical issues, prioritize fixes, and build a 90-day roadmap. You may not see ranking improvements yet, but the foundation is being built.</p>
        <p>In month two, technical fixes go live. The agency resolves crawl errors, improves site speed, optimizes title tags and meta descriptions, and begins on-page improvements. Content creation starts with the highest-priority pages. Early signs of progress may appear for low-competition keywords.</p>
        <p>In month three, content publication accelerates. The agency targets additional keywords, builds initial backlinks, and optimizes local listings if applicable. Rankings begin shifting for target terms. Traffic increases may be modest, but the trajectory becomes visible. By the end of month three, you should have a clear picture of whether the strategy is working.</p>

